{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Pushed as Gerrit change 2659 (`dae0727e1580`), branched from `origin/main` at `f45408b7507a`. NOT submitted and the ticket stays `in_progress` — the operator verifies votes and submits.\n\n## The design question, answered with evidence\n\n`/var/tmp` is a directory on the **root XFS** filesystem (`/dev/nvme0n1p1`). Four candidate byte ceilings were considered; the reasoning is in `infra/scripts/vartmp-cap.sh`'s header so a future reader does not have to reconstruct it:\n\n1. **XFS project quota** — the only real ceiling (kernel `EDQUOT`, no fill-rate assumption). CHOSEN as the ceiling but it is **operator-enabled**. XFS reads quota mount options at MOUNT time and refuses to enable accounting on a remount, so on the ROOT filesystem it requires `rootflags=pquota` in `GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX`, a grub regeneration and a **REBOOT** — a scheduled Gerrit outage a deploy tick may not take, and not something this change may apply. Corroborated by Red Hat's own guidance (\"quota option can not be enabled for non-root XFS filesystem\" by remount; for root, set `rootflags=pquota` in `GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX`). The installer therefore applies the limit only where the kernel is already accounting, and otherwise prints the exact steps.\n2. **`systemd-tmpfiles` age cleanup** — installed, and it bounds **age, never bytes**. Its `q`/`Q` lines carry btrfs qgroup limits and do nothing on XFS, so the approved design's \"tmpfiles age+size\" has no size half available on this filesystem.\n3. **A bounded oldest-first reaper timer** — installed, and it is what holds the budget today.\n4. Rejected, with reasons recorded: a loop-mounted fixed-size image at `/var/tmp` (permanently consumes its size, hides files open across the mount, and a sparse image on a full root turns an ordinary ENOSPC into a shutdown of the image filesystem — and it cannot be validated without applying it to the host); and binding `/var/tmp` onto the S1 gate-scratch volume (moves bytes rather than bounding them, and while that volume is attached-but-unmounted, bug `dcc3-75ee-26ce-4840`, it would fail open onto root — the exact \"bounded on paper\" shape this epic exists to remove).\n\n## What the reaper does NOT guarantee\n\nStated plainly in the script header, the runbook and the commit message: **it is a mitigation with a fill-rate assumption, not a ceiling.** It runs on a 5-minute timer, so between two runs `/var/tmp` is bounded only by the volume and the enforced bound is `cap + fill_rate x interval`. **At the 4 GiB default and a 300 s period, a sustained net fill above ~14.6 MB/s defeats it — and this gp3 volume does 125 MB/s baseline, roughly 8.5x that.** A single runaway writer outruns it; its real job is bounding steady accumulation, which is the shape `/var/tmp` actually had at the outage. Second assumption: nothing younger than `VAR_TMP_MIN_AGE_SECONDS` (900 s) is ever evicted — the snapshot janitor's grace window — so a burst of fresh files is unreclaimable by design, and when it cannot get under the budget it says so rather than exiting quietly.\n\n`var_tmp_hard_quota_in_effect` exists so this is a **reading, not a claim**: the box publishes which regime it is in. It is deliberately NOT alarmed (its honest value is 0 until the reboot is scheduled, and an alarm that pages continuously is muted within a day); `var_tmp_cleanup_active` IS alarmed, because a stale drop-in or a dead timer is the state most likely to be mistaken for a working ceiling.\n\n## Cap derivation\n\n4 GiB (`VAR_TMP_MAX_BYTES`), age 7 d (tightening AL2023's shipped 30 d). 4 GiB sits either side of the 3.6 GiB observed: above it so steady state does not thrash the reaper, and its 85% alarm threshold (3.4 GiB) below it, so this configuration would have NAMED `/var/tmp` before it reached its incident size. That relationship is asserted in `test_the_shipped_ceiling_alarms_below_the_size_var_tmp_reached_at_the_outage` against the value `--print-env` actually ships, not a constant restated in the test file.\n\n## Seeded-defect results — 8/8 caught\n\nAll tests were seen RED first (script absent), then perturbed one at a time and restored:\n\n| Seeded defect | Caught by |\n|---|---|\n| cap widened 4 GiB to 8 GiB | `test_print_env_publishes_the_ceiling…` + `test_the_shipped_ceiling_alarms_below…` |\n| `var_tmp_usage_high` `treat_missing_data` to `notBreaching` | `test_host_published_disk_alarms…` + `test_host_alarms_treat_missing_data…` |\n| `rebar-var-tmp-cleanup-not-active` alarm deleted | `test_the_var_tmp_generator_alarms_exist_and_are_wired_to_sns` + the watched-set equality |\n| reaper drops its grace window | `test_the_reaper_never_evicts_inside_its_grace_window` |\n| reaper stops descending into `rebar-evidence` | `test_evidence_directories_are_reaped_individually_not_as_one_tree` |\n| `cleanup_in_effect` stops checking the timer | `test_the_cleanup_heartbeat_is_zero_when_the_timer_is_not_running` |\n| probe publishes 0 instead of staying silent | 3 tests incl. `test_an_unmeasurable_var_tmp_publishes_nothing_rather_than_zero` |\n| `/var/tmp` `du` loses its `bounded` wrapper | `test_the_var_tmp_walk_is_wall_clock_bounded` |\n\nThe first seed exposed a real weakness: the outage-threshold test originally compared two constants in the test file and passed while the script shipped any other value. It now reads the shipped default from `--print-env`.\n\n## Gate history (verbatim)\n\n`claim` refused before review: *\"cannot start work on 2ba3-bf77-1303-4b2d: no certified plan-review attestation (signature: unsigned; ticket has no signature).\"*\n\nFirst `review-plan` run returned `stale-running` (daemon died before recording a terminal status); restarted. The second run returned **BLOCK** on one finding (`f01d25aefd3a23083`, criterion G6): *\"The plan enforces a byte cap via a cleanup service/timer or quota but does not require a heartbeat/failure signal proving the enforcement mechanism itself is alive, so a silently-failed timer/quota could leave /var/tmp usage reading nominal while nothing is actually enforcing the cap.\"* Plus advisory `fe8823c8dcb52cee1` (E5): tests named config presence rather than enforcement behaviour, and advisory P6 (compound AC lines).\n\nAll three were remediated in the description — an explicit enforcement-alive-heartbeat criterion, an enforcement-behaviour test criterion, split compound criteria, and the resolved design ambiguity with numeric defaults — plus `file_impact` and `verify_commands` corrected to the files actually touched. The re-run **PASSED** and signed at `25d1d683e55a2004ca9bd3dd2244bb42654953de`. No `--force` was used on any surface.\n\n## Verification\n\n`make lint` and `make typecheck` clean; complexity ratchet `new=0 increased=0 stale=16`, mechanism ratchet `new=0 increased=0 stale=0` (the mechanism census does not scan `infra/scripts`, confirmed against the baseline). `terraform fmt -check -recursive` and `terraform validate` both clean — **written and validated, never applied**. 45 new/edited tests green, plus 101 sibling observability/cap tests and 151 infra-shape tests, all under the bare `pytest` console script. Import-convention pre-push check green. `scripts/check_orphaned_load.py` exit 0."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788585480306924139,"uuid":"12db18b0-2402-4602-923b-0c1971de8217"}